<section xml:id="sec-ch03-01-DerivativesVectorValuedFunctions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Derivatives of Vector-Valued Functions</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Given a vector-valued function
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m>, we learned that for
      a specific <m>t</m> value <m>t_{0}, \vec{r}\left(t_{0}\right)</m>
      tells you the location of a particle travelling along the graph of
      <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> at time <m>t_{0}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      What if we want to know how fast and in what direction is the particle
      travelling at a given time? We can answer these questions using
      differentiation.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Theoretical Definition of the Derivative</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a vector-valued function
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m>, defined on some
        open interval, its derivative at <m>t=t_{0}</m> is defined as
        <md>
          \vec{r}^{\prime}\left(t_{0}\right)=\lim _{\Delta t \rightarrow 0} \frac{\vec{r}\left(t_{0}+\Delta t\right)-\vec{r}\left(t_{0}\right)}{\Delta t}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Since <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}\left(t_{0}\right)</m> is defined as a
            limit, it might not exist.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            To evaluate a limit of a vector-valued function, you evaluate the
            limit in each component.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-01-ex-7-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the function
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle t^{3}-1, t+2\right\rangle</m> find:
      </p>

      <p>(a) <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)</m>.</p>

      <p>(b) <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(1)</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the definition of the derivative, evaluating the limit in each
        component:
        <md>
          \vec{r}'(t) = \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\vec{r}(t+\Delta t)-\vec{r}(t)}{\Delta t} = \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\left\langle (t+\Delta t)^3-1, (t+\Delta t)+2 \right\rangle - \left\langle t^3-1, t+2 \right\rangle}{\Delta t}
        </md>
        In the first component, expanding
        <m>(t+\Delta t)^3 = t^3+3t^2\Delta t+3t\Delta t^2+\Delta t^3</m>:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\left( (t+\Delta t)^3-1 \right)-\left( t^3-1 \right)}{\Delta t} = \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{3t^2\Delta t+3t\Delta t^2+\Delta t^3}{\Delta t}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \left( 3t^2+3t\Delta t+\Delta t^2 \right) = 3t^2</mrow>
        </md>
        In the second component:
        <md>
          \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\left( (t+\Delta t)+2 \right)-\left( t+2 \right)}{\Delta t} = \lim_{\Delta t \to 0} \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta t} = 1
        </md>
        Hence <m>\vec{r}'(t) = \left\langle 3t^2, 1 \right\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Evaluating the derivative from part (a) at <m>t=1</m>:
        <md>
          \vec{r}'(1) = \left\langle 3(1)^2, 1 \right\rangle = \langle 3,1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      In Calculus I, <m>f^{\prime}(x)</m> represents (geometrically) a limit
      of secant lines approaching a tangent line. Similarly,
      <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)</m> represents (geometrically) a limit of
      <q>secant</q> vectors approaching a tangent vector.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-01-secant-vectors">
    <caption>From LibreTexts: Calculus, Section 12.2</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_09_10_1e5944898def0022a5afg-2.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A curve in xyz-space with two position vectors drawn from the origin to points on the curve: vector r of t, and vector r of, t plus delta t, at a point further along the curve.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A curve in <m>xyz</m>-space with two position vectors drawn from the origin to points on the curve: <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>, and <m>\vec{r}(t+\Delta t)</m>, at a point further along the curve.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!--\section*{Mathematica Slide.}-->
  <!--\section*{Computational Definition of the Derivative}-->

  <definition>
    <title>Computational Definition of the Derivative</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a vector-valued function
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m>. If <m>f, g</m>,
        and <m>h</m> are all differentiable on the <m>t</m>-interval
        ( <m>a, b</m> ), then <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is differentiable on the
        <m>t</m>-interval ( <m>a, b</m> ), and its derivative is given by
        the vector-valued function
        <md>
          \vec{r}^{\prime}(t)=\left\langle f^{\prime}(t), g^{\prime}(t), h^{\prime}(t)\right\rangle
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-01-ex-7-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle\ln \left(\frac{2t^2}{\pi}-1\right), \frac{t^{3}}{3\pi}, \cos (t+3\pi)\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p>(a) On what interval is <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> differentiable?</p>

      <p>(b) Compute <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(\pi)</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By the computational definition, <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is differentiable
        wherever all three components are differentiable. The components
        <m>\frac{t^3}{3\pi}</m> and <m>\cos(t+3\pi)</m> are differentiable
        for every <m>t</m>. The component
        <m>\ln\left( \frac{2t^2}{\pi}-1 \right)</m> is defined and
        differentiable when the input of the logarithm is positive:
        <md>
          \frac{2t^2}{\pi}-1 \gt 0 \Rightarrow t^2 \gt \frac{\pi}{2} \Rightarrow t \lt -\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}} \ \text{ or } \ t \gt \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}
        </md>
        Hence <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is differentiable on the intervals
        <m>\left( -\infty, -\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}} \right)</m> and
        <m>\left( \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}, \infty \right)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiating each component (using the single-variable chain rule
        in the first and third):
        <md>
          \vec{r}'(t) = \left\langle \frac{\frac{4t}{\pi}}{\frac{2t^2}{\pi}-1}, \frac{t^2}{\pi}, -\sin(t+3\pi) \right\rangle = \left\langle \frac{4t}{2t^2-\pi}, \frac{t^2}{\pi}, -\sin(t+3\pi) \right\rangle
        </md>
        Since <m>\pi \gt \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2}}</m>, the function is
        differentiable at <m>t=\pi</m>, and we can evaluate:
        <md>
          \vec{r}'(\pi) = \left\langle \frac{4\pi}{2\pi^2-\pi}, \frac{\pi^2}{\pi}, -\sin(4\pi) \right\rangle = \left\langle \frac{4}{2\pi-1}, \pi, 0 \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Properties of Derivatives of Vector-Valued Functions</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>\vec{u}(t)</m> and <m>\vec{v}(t)</m> be differentiable
      vector-valued functions and let <m>f(t)</m> be a differentiable
      scalar-valued function, all at the input <m>t</m>. Then
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Constant Rule:
            <md>
              \frac{d}{d t}(\mathrm{c})=0
            </md>
            for any constant vector <m>\vec{c}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Sum Rule:
            <md>
              \frac{d}{d t}(\vec{u}(t)+\vec{v}(t))=\vec{u}^{\prime}(t)+\vec{v}^{\prime}(t)
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Chain Rule:
            <md>
              \frac{d}{d t}[\vec{u}(f(t))]=\vec{u}^{\prime}(f(t)) f^{\prime}(t)
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Dot Product Rule:
            <md>
              \frac{d}{d t}(\vec{u}(t) \cdot \vec{v}(t))=\vec{u}^{\prime}(t) \cdot \vec{v}(t)+\vec{u}(t) \cdot \vec{v}^{\prime}(t)
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Cross Product Rule:
            <md>
              \frac{d}{d t}(\vec{u}(t) \times \vec{v}(t))=\vec{u}^{\prime}(t) \times \vec{v}(t)+\vec{u}(t) \times \vec{v}^{\prime}(t)
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Notation for the Chain Rule:</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When evaluating a derivative of the form
            <m>\frac{d}{d t}[\vec{u}(f(t))]</m>, if <m>f(t) \neq t</m>, then
            <m>\frac{d}{d t}[\vec{u}(f(t))] \neq \vec{u}^{\prime}(f(t))</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>f(t) \neq t</m>, interpret <m>\vec{u}^{\prime}(f(t))</m>
            as <q>take the derivative of <m>\vec{u}</m> with respect to
            <m>t</m>, then replace all <m>t</m> 's with <m>f(t)</m>.</q>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-01-ex-7-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\vec{u}(t)= (t^{2}+3t+1 )\vec{i}-e^{2t} \vec{k}</m>, compute
        <m>\frac{d}{d t}\left[\vec{u}\left(t^{3}\right)\right]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the Chain Rule
        <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left[ \vec{u}(f(t)) \right] = \vec{u}'(f(t))f'(t)</m>
        with <m>f(t) = t^3</m>. First,
        <md>
          \vec{u}'(t) = (2t+3)\vec{i} - 2e^{2t}\vec{k}
        </md>
        Following the note above, <m>\vec{u}'(t^3)</m> means we replace all
        <m>t</m>'s in <m>\vec{u}'(t)</m> with <m>t^3</m>:
        <md>
          \vec{u}'(t^3) = (2t^3+3)\vec{i} - 2e^{2t^3}\vec{k}
        </md>
        Since <m>f'(t) = 3t^2</m>, the Chain Rule gives
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dt}\left[ \vec{u}(t^3) \right] = \left( (2t^3+3)\vec{i} - 2e^{2t^3}\vec{k} \right) 3t^2 = (6t^5+9t^2)\vec{i} - 6t^2e^{2t^3}\vec{k}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle</m>, the
        vector <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}\left(t_{0}\right)</m> is called the
        tangent vector to the curve at <m>t=t_{0}</m>, and is usually plotted
        with starting point at
        <m>\left(f\left(t_{0}\right), g\left(t_{0}\right), h\left(t_{0}\right)\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Below is the graph of
    <m>\vec{r}(t)=\left\langle 3\cos (t), 5\cos\left(\frac{t}{2}\right), \sin (t)\right\rangle, 0 \leq t \leq 4\pi</m>,
    with <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)</m> plotted every <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> units
    of time.
  </p>

